/E (1.1 Background and Motivation)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In modern wireless communication and signal processing applications, accurate signal amplification and strength detection are crucial for reliable system performance. A limiting amplifier is a key component in intermediate frequency \(IF\) stages of communication receivers, ensuring that the signal maintains a constant amplitude regardless of variations in input strength. Similarly, a received signal strength indicator \(RSSI\) plays a vital role in determining the power level of the incoming signal, which is essential for adaptive gain control and efficient data demodulation.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Traditional limiting amplifiers and RSSI circuits often face challenges related to power consumption, voltage constraints, and accuracy. As modern electronic devices move towards portable and battery-operated applications, low-voltage and low-power designs are in high demand. Implementing these circuits using CMOS \(Complementary Metal-Oxide-Semiconductor\) technology provides a promising solution due to its power efficiency and compatibility with integrated circuit fabrication.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This project focuses on designing and implementing a 2-V, 10.7-MHz CMOS limiting amplifier and RSSI, optimizing for low power consumption, high gain, and precision. The circuit employs innovative design techniques such as folded diode loads for low-voltage operation and cross-connected source-coupled pairs for offset reduction. The goal is to develop a robust and efficient circuit that meets the requirements of modern IF applications in wireless communication systems, RF receivers, and mobile devices.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
